            <div xml:id="d74">

                <pb facs="0124" n="114" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.796/5–2345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-23T14:00:00+07:00">May 23, 1945—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>832. Appropriate officer at U. S. Army Headquarters here states that sending of
                    two pilots to China to assist CATC was disapproved because of tightness of
                    transportation and lack of adequate number of planes of CATC. (ReDeptel 578,
                    April 13, 4 p.m.) He states this action was taken without knowledge of CNAC
                    officials and that when transport situation is easier and when CATC has more
                    planes headquarters <pb facs="0125" n="115" xml:id="pg115"/> is prepared to
                    reconsider matter. (According to Communications Ministry, CATC during 1944
                    operated only two old planes on irregular schedules.)</p>
                <p>Regarding second paragraph of Depts telegram, officer stated that there was no
                    question of favoring CNAC and American company interested in that concern. He
                    said that he recognized importance from long-range standpoint of having another
                    American company assist Chinese in development of civil aviation here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
